  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea is to optimize the autocorrelation by modeling the constraint problem as a linear program (LP) and using exponential splitting to create asymmetric solutions. This method exploits the fact that feasible sequences are limited by their autocorrelation and finds sequences that tighten this bound. Starting from a parent sequence, we iteratively split it into two parts (P and N) whose exponential combinations (P and N) minimize the objective. Each split concentrates energy in specific frequencies to reduce autocorrelation peaks. This approach balances between maintaining positive values and creating gaps to avoid destructive interference. The benefit is clearer frequency-energy control than previous methods. However, it sacrifices some theoretical guarantees for better practical bounds.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe have a sequence P\u2099 of nonnegative length N=512. The objective to minimize is:\n    f(P) = (2N * max\u2096(convolve(P,P))[k]) / (sum(P))\u00b2\n\nBy introducing a split, we project onto the feasible set constrained by:\n  ||convolve(P, P)||\u221e \u2264 M\nand\n  P \u2265 0\n\nThe variables are:\n- P: the decision variables (coefficients to optimize, 0 \u2264 P\u1d62 \u2264 1000)\n- M: the maximum autocorrelation bound from the oracle\n\nThe LP formulation for a given P\u2080 finds the \"antiperiod\" Q\u2099 such that:\n  minimize \u27e8\u2211, Q\u27e9\n  subject to:\n    ||convolve(P\u2080, Q)||\u221e \u2264 M - \u03b5\u2080\n    Q \u2265 0\n\nThen the new sequence P\u2082 is recovered by:\n  P\u2082 = (P\u2082\u208b\u2081exp\u2070 + P\u2082\u208b\u2081exp\u00b9) at each position, where exponent splits the \"size\" budget\n\nThis effectively moves energy away from the center to minimize the autocorrelation bound.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Start by loading the parent sequence (if available) or initialize with a uniform sequence.\n\n2. Set parameters:\n   - Resolution = 512\n   - Autocorrelation tolerance (\u03b5) = 0.05\n   - Split ratio = 0.8\n\n3. For each iteration:\n   a. Use `build_A` to construct the constraint matrix from the current sequence P\u2080.\n   b. Use `linprog` to solve the LP to find Q (antiperiod) that ties up energy.\n   c. Form the next candidate P\u2081 = PPrev \u2a02 P_prev (exponential cross) but note exponential splitting:\n        For each lag, the weight is chosen between P\u2080 and Q by an interpolating exponent.\n\n4. When updating sequences:\n   - Save the best candidate found using the authoritative evaluator\n   - Maintain strict bounds (0 to 1000) and nonnegative coefficients\n\n5. Exit conditions: \n   - Hit 450 iterations or 30 minutes before timeout\n   - Observe stabilization of the objective\n   - Switch to structured local search if failures occur\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Variations include:\n  1. Local search (anisotropic smoothing) around the solution after an LP failure\n  2. Dynamic energy splitting (focus varies by 15% from the threshold)\n  3. Coefficient saturation (exponentially damp energy concentration)\n- Defaults:\n  - Max iterations: 450\n  - Time budget: 300 seconds\n  - Step size: 0.2 (mutates the energy profile)\n- Trigger adjustments for:\n  - Divergence: revert to a local optimum solution\n  - Convergence: switch to structured annealing schedule\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Estimated cost:\n  - Build_A: O(N\u00b3) with N=512 (approx 512\u00b2 * 50 = 13 million operations)\n  - `linprog`: O(N\u00b3) with 512 variables (heavy)\n- Time schedule:\n  - Wait 10 seconds for build_A\n  - Allow 25 seconds per LP solution\n  - Monitor progress every 5 iterations\n- Resource management:\n  - Clip temporary storage to persistent matrices\n  - Migrate convolutions to FFT implementation for length >256\n  - Initialize fallback using `_upscale` from parent vector\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Each candidate subject to:\n  1. Critique by `normalize` (bounds 0 to 1000, sum \u2265 0.01)\n  2. Discharge by `evaluate_sequence` (full autocorrelation check)\n  3. Check for specific errors: \n        inf, NaN, length mismatch\n  - Return only sequences with np.isfinite(score) and score < 1.50274349\n  - Fallback: returned parent sequence if none found better than 1.520654\n\nRepresentation and format are consistent with parent vector. The default initial convolve operation is full, mode=\"valid\", and uses Pytons capped Simpson rule.",
